            <ArticleTitle>6-Acetylmorphine detected in the absence of morphine in pain management patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>749-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/FTD.0b013e3181b9da04</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry was used to identify and confirm the presence of 6-acetylmorphine and morphine in 22,361 urines of pain management patients. Thirty urines tested positive for 6-acetylmorphine above a cutoff of 10 ng/mL. Twenty-three percent of the patients with urinary concentrations of 6-acetylmorphine above 10 ng/mL had urinary morphine concentrations below 300 ng/mL.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
